  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased capacity of national institutions to fight against child labour in Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria is one of the countries with high population within the West Africa region, with an estimated population of over 190 million with 54.4% of this population (or 76 million people) live in poverty. The National survey on Child labour 2022 stated that 62.9 million children (5-17 years) live in Nigeria, representing 30.3 percent of the population (with 62.2 percent in Rural areas and 37.6 percent in Urban areas). 31.7 million children (50.5%) of the children are in Economic Activity, 24.6 million children (39.2%) of children are in child labour, and 14.3 million children (22.9%) are in hazardous work. This highlights the need for concerted efforts to ensure the elimination of child labour in the country. The ILO is supporting the National Bureau of Statistics to analyse the child labour dataset from the 2024 Labour force survey, thus, in 2026, there will be an updated national Child labour report. The ILO strategy seeks to strengthen policy and the legal framework in Nigeria to create an enabling environment for the elimination of child labour. The third phase of the National Child Labour Policy and the National Action Plan for the elimination of Child labour validated. In 2013, The National Steering Committee for the Elimination of Child Labour developed a risk assessment report on hazardous child labour work, but it does not detail the specific types of hazardous activity that are prohibited by the Government, thus, the ILO, support, the Nigeria Hazardous Work List prohibited for persons under 18 will be developed. To support the achievement of SDG 8.7, the second phase of the Alliance 8.7 RoadMap will be developed and the Alliance 8.7 network in Nigeria will be supported to effectively implement the roadmap. The 2025 annual implementation report for Alliance 8.7 will be developed and submitted within agreed timelines</narrative>
  </description>
